417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1031 "Converting Between Mixed Units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1033 2233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050363 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "42e7a396-f7f5-5ef7-82f9-ae4f6cfc0ca0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1031 "Writing Ratios Using Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1033 552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4d30ea10-ea6d-511a-a7e5-cce5fffa2a64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073570 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073570 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the missing number in the statement below?\n\n$\\qquad$ $16$ chickens in $2$ chicken coops $0$ chickens per chicken coop." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "579bda4d-058c-5572-8d5d-addf9650d1ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073570 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of the number of chickens to the number of chicken coops is ${\\color{blue}16}:{\\color{red}2}$. So we start with the ratio $16:2$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}16}}{{\\color{red}2}}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{16 \\div 2}{2 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{8}{1} = 8\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$\\qquad$ $16$ chickens in $2$ chicken coops $= 8$ chickens per chicken coop." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073571 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073571 #edb/ref 1071 "Carl walks $450$ meters in $5$ minutes. If he walks at a constant pace, at what rate does he walk?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073571 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "199c8a83-265f-598b-a6f3-a606f3bbd7a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073571 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of meters to minutes that Carl walks is ${\\color{blue}450}:{\\color{red}5}$. So we start with the ratio $450:5$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}450}}{{\\color{red}5}}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}450}\\div 5}{{\\color{red}5}\\div 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}90}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Carl walks at a rate of $90$ meters per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073572 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073572 #edb/ref 1071 "Anthony says $400$ words in $6$ minutes. If he speaks at a steady pace, what statement describes the unit rate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073572 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3997c678-94c6-5f9f-bf6a-dc752e89b3fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073572 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of spoken words to minutes is ${\\color{blue}400}:{\\color{red}6}$. So we start with the ratio $400:6$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}400}}{{\\color{red}6}}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce this fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{400 \\div 2}{6 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{200}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Anthony said\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{200}{3}\n$$\n\nwords per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073573 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073573 #edb/ref 1071 "Donald writes $3$ words in $2$ seconds. If he writes at a steady pace, how many words does he write per second?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ffe79c28-3eef-51a9-9185-283676fdaf65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073573 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of words to seconds is ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}2}$. Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}2}}\n$$\n\nWe can convert this fraction to a decimal by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\times 5}{2 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{15}{10} = 1.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, Donald writes $1.5$ words per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073886 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073886 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Missing Value in a Unit Rate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073886 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073886 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2a5b57e9-71fa-5b06-9984-c18e20f79e4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073887 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Whole Number Unit Rates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073887 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073887 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f78d8f50-ae86-59e6-8c88-abc42a6a9fc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073888 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073888 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Unit Rates as Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073888 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073888 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6edca753-d8cd-55a6-883b-81f80af0e0cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073889 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073889 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073889 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073889 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073889 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Unit Rates as Decimals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073889 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073889 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f84a62df-3608-5166-af37-4f72d8f1b821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073975 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073975 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073975 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073975 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073975 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073975 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073975 #edb/ref 1045 "Unit Rates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073975 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073975 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073975 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4bb23d88-5fbe-55d0-9559-771761b8bf07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074881 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074881 #edb/ref 1061 7810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074881 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3556c71-dd79-50f3-ae2c-c8fc08b7ffba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074881 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074882 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074882 #edb/ref 1061 7812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074882 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a7c7fe4-b762-5013-ae10-c779da960528" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074882 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074883 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074883 #edb/ref 1061 7813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fcef112f-4080-5902-94fc-fae7173bec2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074884 #edb/ref 1061 7814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35d428fd-4121-5352-b3c4-7b020ac1f590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074885 #edb/ref 1061 7815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dae52be5-aebb-5a56-9fa4-1d51a09e8010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074970 #edb/ref 1064 "A **unit rate** is a comparison of two quantities where one of the terms has a quantity of $1$. This means the ratio, in fraction form, has a denominator of $1$.\n\nFor instance, suppose Michael earned $\\[math] 200$ by selling $4$ identical tires.\n\nThe ratio of dollars to tires is ${\\color{blue}200}:{\\color{red}4}$. Writing the ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200}}{{\\color{red}4}} \\,\n$$\n\nThe unit rate is how many dollars Michael got for $1$ tire.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200} \\div 4}{{\\color{red}4} \\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}50}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nThis is the unit rate. In this case, we say that Michael got $\\color{blue}50$ dollars *per* tire." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074970 #edb/ref 1065 2055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074970 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0bf127db-09c1-53b5-b2fa-6f6520b032f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074970 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
